{"causes": ["The political instability and decline of the Early Lê dynasty created a power vacuum in Đại Việt.", "Lý Công Uẩn's strategic position as a high-ranking official and commander in the Lê court allowed him to garner support.", "The desire for a more stable and centralized government after decades of internal strife spurred his ascent to power."], "effects": ["Lý Thái Tổ's reign initiated the Lý dynasty, which lasted over 200 years and brought significant stability to Đại Việt.", "The relocation of the capital to Thăng Long (Hanoi) established a lasting political and cultural center for Vietnam.", "His promotion of Buddhism fostered cultural unity and contributed to the development of Vietnamese arts and literature."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "ly-thai-to", "sourceName": "Ly Thai To", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "ly-dynasty", "targetName": "Lý Dynasty", "context": "Lý Thái Tổ founded the Lý dynasty in Đại Việt in 1009, establishing a new imperial lineage."}, {"sourceSlug": "ly-thai-to", "sourceName": "Ly Thai To", "verb": "TRANSFORMS", "targetSlug": "dai-co-viet", "targetName": "Đại Cồ Việt", "context": "Lý Thái Tổ transformed Đại Cồ Việt from a fragmented state into a centralized imperial power through administrative reforms and a new capital."}, {"sourceSlug": "le-ngoa-trieu", "sourceName": "Lê Ngọa Triều", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "ly-thai-to", "targetName": "Ly Thai To", "context": "The death of Lê Ngọa Triều, the last emperor of the Early Lê dynasty, in 1009 created the political opportunity for Lý Công Uẩn to become emperor Lý Thái Tổ."}, {"sourceSlug": "ly-thai-to", "sourceName": "Ly Thai To", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "thang-long-hanoi", "targetName": "Thăng Long (Hanoi)", "context": "Lý Thái Tổ famously moved the capital of Đại Việt to Đại La in 1010, renaming it Thăng Long, where many of his foundational reforms occurred."}, {"sourceSlug": "ly-thai-to", "sourceName": "Ly Thai To", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "buddhism-in-vietnam", "targetName": "Buddhism in Vietnam", "context": "Lý Thái Tổ significantly influenced the development of Buddhism in Vietnam by promoting it as a state religion and sponsoring numerous pagodas during his reign."}], "places": [{"name": "Hoa Lư, Đại Việt", "role": "Former Capital"}, {"name": "Thăng Long (Hanoi), Đại Việt", "role": "New Capital"}, {"name": "Đại Việt", "role": "Imperial Realm"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Ly Thai To", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "ly-thai-to"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q716945", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%BD_Th%C3%A1i_T%E1%BB%95"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Lý Thái Tổ's founding of the Lý dynasty and the establishment of Thăng Long as capital fundamentally shaped Vietnamese statehood and culture for centuries, setting the stage for two major dynasties.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-23T07:02:24Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29986699140", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Lý Thái Tổ, personal name Lý Công Uẩn (李公蘊), was the founding emperor of the Lý dynasty. He reigned the empire for 18 years, from 1009 until his death in 1028.", "newValue": "Lý Thái Tổ (personal name Lý Công Uẩn), reigning from 1009 to 1028, was the pivotal founding emperor of the Lý dynasty, which ushered in a period of unprecedented stability and cultural development for Đại Việt (modern Vietnam). His ascent marked a definitive break from the fragmented post-Tang period, establishing a centralized imperial system that would endure for over two centuries. His most enduring decision, the relocation of the capital from Hoa Lư to Đại La, which he renamed Thăng Long (Ascending Dragon), strategically positioned the heart of the empire for future growth and solidified the nation's independence from Chinese influence.

Following the death of Lê Ngọa Triều, the last emperor of the Early Lê dynasty, in 1009, Lý Công Uẩn, then a high-ranking official, was acclaimed emperor by the court and populace, taking the regnal name Lý Thái Tổ. His reign was characterized by crucial administrative reforms aimed at consolidating power and establishing a stable governance structure. He implemented a new legal code, the Hình Thư, and reorganized the military, strengthening the nation's defenses. Furthermore, Lý Thái Tổ actively promoted Buddhism as a state religion, commissioning numerous pagodas and sponsoring monastic orders, which fostered cultural unity and intellectual development across Đại Việt.

The Lý dynasty, founded by Lý Thái Tổ, inherited a relatively nascent independent state and transformed it into a formidable regional power. His strategic move of the capital to Thăng Long proved instrumental for the subsequent 200-year flourishing of the Lý and Trần dynasties, establishing a political and cultural center that remains vital today. The administrative infrastructure and cultural policies he initiated laid the groundwork for Đại Việt's long-term autonomy and distinctive identity, resisting later attempts at Chinese domination and fostering a unique Vietnamese imperial tradition."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-23T07:02:24Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29986699140", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "974-03-13, Từ Sơn"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "974-03-13, Từ Sơn"}], "newValue": ["The political instability and decline of the Early Lê dynasty created a power vacuum in Đại Việt.", "Lý Công Uẩn's strategic position as a high-ranking official and commander in the Lê court allowed him to garner support.", "The desire for a more stable and centralized government after decades of internal strife spurred his ascent to power."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-23T07:02:24Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29986699140", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1028"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1028"}], "newValue": ["Lý Thái Tổ's reign initiated the Lý dynasty, which lasted over 200 years and brought significant stability to Đại Việt.", "The relocation of the capital to Thăng Long (Hanoi) established a lasting political and cultural cente